ople  seem to comply with most of the social  distancing orders the closure orders etc  the one thing that really got people  angry in 1918 were in those communities  that implemented mass mandates a number  of communities did especially out west  denver seattle san francisco oakland etc  and in those communities we have ample  evidence that there were many people who  did not wear their masks and there were  lots of others who had their masks with  them and maybe thought they could get  away without wearing it  and were caught by police officers and  in most of those cases they were  arrested and fined  in san francisco  there was there were two masked mandates  when the epidemic was not over and there  was another surge they implemented  another round of mass mandates but no  closure orders and that second round of  mass mandates really angered a lot of  people so much so that an anti-mass  league was formed and about 2 000 people  met uh to complain about the masks and  to listen to  uh lectures by some physicians in the  area you said they didn't work  politicians who said it was an  infringement on the civil liberties all  of the same types of things that we're  hearing today  and what similarities do you see between  then and now in terms of this resistance  to mass mandates and of course now the  vaccine mandates what does that come out  of  well i think there's a deep-seated um  you know sort of social understanding in  america that we we hold our our personal  freedoms our individual freedoms very  dearly um and of course people did in  1918 as well and so there's a there's  resistance to you know the notion of the  government telling you or forcing  someone to do something that they don't  want and that's understandable i think  the big difference though uh between  1918 and today is that that resistance  in 1918  was by and large not political it may  have been ideological but it wasn't  partisan there were people who were both  republicans and democrats 